  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread showers continue early this morning \nthen taper off later today; rain lingers into this evening in \nthe southeast. \n\nOverrunning moisture to the north of developing weak low pressure \nhas brought showers to much of the area early this morning. Latest \nradar shows widespread rain, mostly light, along and east of the I-\n95 with scattered showers elsewhere. There are some pockets of \nmoderate rainfall which are mostly in SE VA and NE NC. Showers will \ncontinue across most of the area through the early morning hours. \nThe UL trough over the east coast will slide offshore later this \nmorning as sfc high pressure tries to move in from the NW. This will \nusher in drier air and limit most of the rain to the SE closer to \nthe area of low pressure. Showers could potentially linger in the \nfar southeast into the evening hours depending on when the low and \nthe front it sits on finally get kicked offshore. Dry conditions are \nthen expected overnight. Cooler temperatures continue today with \nhighs around 80 along and north of I-64 and in the mid to upper 70s \nsouth.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry with just isolated late day \nshowers/storms Sunday/Monday, with higher rain chances Tuesday into \nWednesday; strong to severe storms are possible Tuesday. \n\nNear-average temperatures return Sunday through next week; highs \nwill be in the upper 80s and low 90s. A UL trough axis sets up just \noffshore for most of the week which will put the local forecast area \nunder NW flow. Multiple shortwaves coming down the trough and a cold \nfront will lead to an overall unsettled pattern for much of the \nweek. Sunday/Sunday night should be mostly dry with the exception of \nisolated/widely scattered evening storms over the piedmont. Models \nhave trended stormier for Monday afternoon/evening, so now have \nchance PoPs for most of the FA. Being under NW flow, there is \ncertainly the possibility of a few stronger storms Monday. However, \nthe main show still looks to be the cold front Tues. With the \nstrength of trough, and even a potential sfc low <1000mb, there will \nbe the potential for severe weather, especially if the timing lines \nup with the greatest instability. The 00z ECMWF has a very \ninteresting idea for Tues into Wed with the front's parent low down \nto 995mb stacked under a closed UL low that dips as far south as PA. \nThat would be rather anomalous for July and the ECMWF is the only \nglobal model anywhere near that extreme. Will be interested to see \nhow the models trend going forward for sure. The SPC still has the \nFA highlighted in the extended severe outlook.",
